%erg 0.1
Title: Claim ticket numbers in tickets/.claims/NNNN so git detects a collision
Created: 2026-10-21
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-10-21T15:17Z MinhHaDuong created
2026-11-08T20:52Z MinhHaDuong amended design after review and a 16-repo collision survey (see Evidence, Design (A) marks)

--- body ---
## Context
Asked by the author (2026-10-21), from the IDH harness repo, where the collision had to be policed by a CI job
(`cross-pr-ticket-collision`: fail a pull request whose added ticket ID another open pull request also adds) that
was switched off the same day with GitHub Actions. Evidence of the pain: `docs/erg-manual.md` (ID allocation is
optimistic: two concurrent invocations in different checkouts can take the same number), the existing
post-check retry loop, the 200 ms "branch-tip scan exceeded" warning of `erg new`, and the harness's own CI
guard built for it (git-erg#282). Today two branches that each add `tickets/NNNN-slug-a.erg` and
`tickets/NNNN-slug-b.erg` have different paths, so git sees no conflict; the duplicate surfaces only when the
corpus check (`erg check`) runs on the merged tree.

What the claim adds over the existing checks: the pre-commit hook does not run on a rebase or a forge-side
merge, CI checks a merge ref that goes stale when a sibling lands, and the cross-PR job compares open pull
requests only with each other. An add/add conflict needs none of them: the forge recomputes mergeability
when the base moves, a local rebase stops, and it works offline and on any forge.

## Evidence (2026-11-08 survey of the 16 ticket stores on padme)
Method: git rename detection for ticket files whose number changed (collisions caught and renumbered),
and `ls-tree` of every first-parent default-branch commit for two ticket files sharing a number
(collisions that reached the default branch). Positive control: a duplicate made in a scratch repo was
found. Duplicate counts are an upper bound: arena's one hit (1048, 2026-10-15) is a slug rename that left
the old file behind for one commit, not a collision.

| Repo | Renumbers (last) | Duplicate IDs on the default branch (last) |
|---|---|---|
| AEDIST-technical-report | 54 (2026-06-16) | 34 (2026-06-16) |
| climate-finance-het | 29 (2026-10-23) | 6 (2026-07-27) |
| IDH harness | 12 (2026-10-01) | 0 |
| search-works-for-zotero | 6 (2026-09-09) | 5 (2026-09-09) |
| chemin-de-voix | 1 (2026-04-29) | 6 (2026-05-17) |
| padme | 1 (2026-08-21, a migrate) | 1 (2026-06-09) |
| sen-causal-reading | 1 (2026-11-06) | 0 |
| archiveCIRED | 1 (2026-06-24) | 0 |
| git-erg, arena, DOIfetch, fuzzy-corpus, cadens, JETP-observer | 0 | 0 |

Reading: collisions reached a default branch about 50 times from April to 2026-09-09 (commit messages
confirm "main was red on a duplicate ID" in search-works-for-zotero and climate-finance-het), and none
since. They still happen: two since the harness's CI job was switched off on 2026-10-21
(climate-finance-het 2650->2700 on 2026-10-23, sen-causal-reading 0099->0102 on 2026-11-06), both caught
before merge. So the current gates detect; what remains is the renumber cost, which the claim does not
reduce, and the gates' dependence on CI and agent discipline, which the claim removes. Why duplicates
stopped reaching main after September is not established.

## Design
Amended 2026-11-08 after a review of the author's design (2026-10-21); the changes are marked (A).

- `erg new NNNN` also writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN`, a file of exactly one line: the ticket's slug.
- Two branches claiming the same number create the same path with different content: git reports an add/add
  conflict when the second one is merged or rebased. Tested 2026-11-08 in a scratch repo: merge and rebase
  conflict on the claim; a different number merges clean.
- Two claimants with the same number and the same slug also have the same ticket path; their ticket files
  differ (Created, Author, log), so git conflicts on the ticket file itself. The claim needs nothing more.
- Claims are never deleted, neither by `erg close` nor by `erg rm`: the directory is the record of numbers
  ever used. A claim without a ticket is therefore legitimate, not an error. (A: the earlier text both kept
  claims forever and reported a claim without a ticket.)
- Allocation stays dense: `erg new` takes one above the highest number among the tickets and the claims
  (base, sibling worktrees, branch tips, as today). (A: holes are never reused. The lowest hole is as
  contended as the frontier, and a hole is usually a number an abandoned branch used, still cited by
  branch names, pull request titles and commit messages; reusing it makes `git log --grep NNNN` ambiguous.)
- Recovery: the later claimant renumbers to a number well clear of the high-water mark, per
  `erg integration`, not to the next free one. (A: the earlier text said "next free number", the most
  contended seat, which `erg integration` documents as the cause of a three-step collision chain.) The
  rename covers the claim, the ticket file, its log, references from other tickets on the branch
  (`Blocked-by:`, body), code comments and config that cite the number (climate-finance-het's
  2650->2700 renumber edited two code files), the branch name, and the pull request's title and close
  claim: a stale "Closes NNNN" would close the winner's ticket at merge.
- A slug rename must rewrite the claim too; a rename that leaves the old file behind (arena, 2026-10-15)
  then fails the slug-mismatch check below.

### Backstops (A: required, not optional)
The conflict can be silenced: a merge with `-X ours`, a conflict resolved by keeping one side, or a
`merge=union` attribute on the claims all merge clean (tested 2026-11-08: `-X ours` lands both
`NNNN-*.erg` files under one claim; union writes a two-line claim). `erg check` must therefore reject:
- a claim that is not exactly one line;
- a claim whose slug does not match its ticket file's slug;
- a ticket without a claim (after migration), with an error that prints the plain-file fix
  (`echo SLUG > tickets/.claims/NNNN`), so a hand-made ticket still needs no binary (Agnostic invariant);
- the existing duplicate-ID check stays.

### Staging (A)
`erg new` does not commit, and agents add the ticket by name (`git add tickets/NNNN-*` misses
`tickets/.claims/NNNN`, tested). The claim then sits untracked: `erg check` passes locally because the file
is on disk, and only a fresh clone in CI fails. So:
- `erg new` prints the `git add` line naming both files;
- the pre-commit hook rejects a staged ticket file whose claim is not in the index;
- `erg new` runs `git check-ignore` on the claim path and refuses when it is ignored (some repos, the IDH
  harness, ignore everything and allowlist paths).

### Migration
The migration step (`erg migrate`, or its successor) writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN` with the slug for every
existing ticket, open and closed, so the holes are real holes from the first day. It is idempotent. (A) When
two tickets already share a number, it writes no claim for that number, reports both files and exits
non-zero: one claim cannot hold two slugs.

### Docs (A)
`erg integration` says "No reservation machinery exists or is planned": reword it to describe the claims.

### Not in this ticket
Detection happens when the second claimant reaches the base, not at allocation. The stronger option for a
central origin (push an atomic ref `refs/erg/id/NNNN`, git-erg#282) needs the network.

Per `AGENTS.md` § Earning a feature the burden is on adding: if collisions stop biting, the ticket can be
closed unbuilt.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `erg new` writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN` (one line, the slug), prints the `git add` line for both
      files, and refuses an ignored claim path; `erg close` and `erg rm` leave the claim in place.
- [ ] A test with two branches adding the same number with different slugs: merge and rebase report an
      add/add conflict on the claim file (positive control); claims with different numbers merge clean
      (negative control).
- [ ] `erg new` allocates above the highest ticket or claim number and never reuses a hole.
- [ ] `erg check` rejects a multi-line claim, a slug mismatch, and a ticket without a claim (after
      migration, with the plain-file fix in the message); a claim without a ticket passes. Tests cover the
      `-X ours` and `merge=union` silencings.
- [ ] The pre-commit hook rejects a staged ticket whose claim is not staged.
- [ ] The migration populates the claims for all existing tickets (open and closed), is idempotent, and
      refuses a number two tickets share.
- [ ] The manual's ID-allocation section, `erg integration` (recovery: rename scope, claims) and
      `tickets/AGENTS.md` describe the claims and the rename step.
- [ ] `make check` passes; the bootstrap binary is rebuilt by the normal route.
